{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact head dd8772aea1f721b687ad1bb5ca218debb3ce3558. The five findings in the prior ratified review are resolved: the note now uses a final-fact decision function with mutually exclusive total guards and model tests; specifies a frozen oracle, Projector seam and planted-defect proof; scopes retirement involution; covers six adversarial families with explicit oracles; and accurately bounds Option C to a closed host vocabulary with can/cannot examples. The no-big-bang-rewrite recommendation, measured counts and Tailapps comparison remain sound. Independent gates passed: branch equals origin, only the note differs from current main a30406b3, diff check and clean tree passed, make docs passed, and go test ./internal/workroom -count=1 passed. Architecture: research note only; no layer contract changes. Its staged Go closure-table direction preserves layer 5 and correctly keeps workroom independent of Tailapps/JSONata. Security: no executable change, and keeping act/fact/authority/merge semantics in host Go is sound, but Option C exposes one authority-adjacent defect described below. Simplification: the decision-table extraction is a useful bounded simplification, but S0's oracle is presently much more expensive than the note claims. Two blockers: (1) S0 requires RenderJSON at all 17,123 prefixes. The current final JSON is 40,300,446 bytes and one cached status JSON render takes about 0.37 seconds locally. Re-rendering a growing whole projection at every prefix is O(n^2), plausibly hundreds of GB of serialization and roughly an hour before the candidate/pre-change double run. Replace this with an explicitly bounded checkpoint/sample oracle plus on-failure localization or another measured scheme; state runtime/storage ceilings and prove the planted defect still lands in the sample or triggers bisection. Do not call every-prefix replay cheap. (2) Option C says authority is non-declarable but lets a declaration choose arbitrary terminal flags and waiting-party selectors, and its examples let stale outrank reported or an artifact become reported in a no-merge workroom. Current actionable/terminal status classification feeds unsettled commitment and merge-accounting behavior, so a policy could hide live work or manufacture terminality without a host-certified report/ratification, merge receipt, abandonment or succession. Make host-computed semantic lifecycle class, terminality and accountable party immutable facts; constrain declarations to labels/partitions that cannot change closure or merge protection, or specify and prove an equally strict load-time validator. Include the unratified-artifact and stale-reported counterexamples. No other changes requested.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01298fa2e23cda3f9c66e281c0bea86ad426c886","head":"dd8772aea1f721b687ad1bb5ca218debb3ce3558","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7e826647dd8582dbe98483b9d202ca44dcff51cc\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7e826647dd8582dbe98483b9d202ca44dcff51cc","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}